Overfull \hbox (6438.33551pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 37649--37649
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/12 We resolve an open question about the Berggren ternary tree o
f primitive Pythagorean triples: *Can one navigate directly to a triple involvi
ng a specific prime factor p, without enumerating the tree?* The answer is **ye
s**, through what we call the **Gaussian GPS**  a direct correspondence between
 Gaussian integer factorizations, continued fractions, and Berggren tree paths.
 For primes $p \equiv 1 \pmod{4}$, the tree path to the unique primitive triple
 with hypotenuse $p$ is computable in $O(\log^2 p)$ arithmetic operations via C
ornacchia's algorithm and the **Three-Zone Descent**, a piecewise Möbius transf
ormation we identify as a base-2 variant of the Gauss continued fraction map. W
e prove that the first-branch distribution of hypotenuse primes is predicted by
 Hecke's equidistribution theorem for Gaussian primes, and that the descent map
 has the silver ratio $1 + \sqrt{2}$ as its unique fixed point, with the golden
 ratio $\varphi$ exhibiting a period-2 orbit. All core results are formalized i
n Lean 4 with Mathlib and validated computationally for all primitive triples w
ith hypotenuse below 10,000.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1096.92986pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 37655--37655
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/12 The Berggren tree (Berggren 1934, Barning 1963, Hall 1970) is
 a ternary tree that generates **all** primitive Pythagorean triples from the r
oot $(3, 4, 5)$ through three matrix transformations $A$, $B$, $C$. Each primit
ive triple appears exactly once.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (874.65776pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 37657--37657
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/12 In prior work, we established the **Depth-Factor Theorem**: f
or a semiprime $n = pq$, the "factor-$p$" triple lives at depth $(q-3)/2$ in th
e tree, always along a pure-$A$ path. This raised the tantalizing question:[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (164.53241pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 37659--37659
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/12 \begin{quote}\textit{**Can one navigate to the FACTOR-$p$ sub
tree without enumeration?**}\end{quote}[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (207.75743pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 37663--37663
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/12 1. **For hypotenuse primes** ($p \equiv 1 \pmod{4}$): **Yes**
, in $O(\log^2 p)$ time, via the Gaussian GPS.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (355.9575pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 37665--37665
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/12 2. **For leg primes** (any odd prime $p$): The path is always
