Overfull \hbox (3400.23401pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 48321--48321
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/12 Our central result, the **Fundamental Theorem of Search Duali
ty**, establishes a precise asymmetry: for any fixed target, there exists a sea
rch strategy that finds it (the attractor principle); but for any fixed search 
strategy and any finite horizon, there exists a target that evades all queries 
(the repulsor principle). We further prove that repulsors *require adaptation* 
 no fixed point can serve as a universal evader  while attractors require adapt
ation in the search strategy. This reveals a deep structural asymmetry: **the p
ower to find and the power to hide are not symmetric; they depend on who gets t
o adapt.**[] 
